1
00:00:00,366 --> 00:00:03,866
我先说一件你大概率没认真想过的小事
Let me tell you something you've probably never really thought about

2
00:00:03,866 --> 00:00:06,233
你在取款机上输密码的时候
When you type your PIN at an ATM

3
00:00:06,233 --> 00:00:08,433
有没有低头看过那几个按键
have you ever looked down at those keys

4
00:00:09,066 --> 00:00:10,633
几乎每个数字键上
On almost every number key

5
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:12,600
都有一排凸起的小点
there's a row of raised dots

6
00:00:13,166 --> 00:00:13,966
那是盲文
That's Braille

7
00:00:13,966 --> 00:00:15,833
很多人第一次注意到
The first time people notice it

8
00:00:16,466 --> 00:00:18,166
心里都会冒出一个问题
a question pops into their head

9
00:00:18,266 --> 00:00:20,566
盲人会自己一个人来取款吗
Would a blind person come withdraw cash alone

10
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:22,133
就算他能摸出数字
Even if they can feel out the numbers

11
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:24,200
他怎么知道屏幕上写了什么
how would they know what's on the screen

12
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,566
怎么知道走到的是哪台机器
or which machine they walked up to

13
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:28,066
光靠几个凸点
Just a few dots

14
00:00:28,066 --> 00:00:31,000
好像根本搞不定取钱这一整件事
hardly seem enough to handle the whole thing

15
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:33,600
所以不少人下意识就会觉得
So a lot of people instinctively think

16
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:34,966
这不就是做个样子吗
isn't this just for show

17
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:36,166
摆在那儿好看
just sitting there looking nice

18
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:37,900
白白浪费钱
a waste of money

19
00:00:38,666 --> 00:00:41,366
但我要告诉你一个正好反过来的事实
But here's a fact that's exactly the opposite

20
00:00:41,366 --> 00:00:42,966
就算真的很少有人用
Even if very few people use it

21
00:00:42,966 --> 00:00:44,533
把这排盲文留着
keeping this Braille

22
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:46,733
反而是更省钱的做法
is actually the cheaper choice

23
00:00:46,733 --> 00:00:47,833
真正贵的
What's really expensive

24
00:00:48,366 --> 00:00:51,100
是专门去做一批不带盲文的键盘
is making a separate batch of keypads without Braille

25
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,566
你先别急着反驳
Don't rush to argue yet

26
00:00:53,566 --> 00:00:56,233
这条视频就从这一个反差讲起
This video starts from this one contrast

27
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:57,066
我们总以为
We always assume

28
00:00:57,066 --> 00:00:59,166
用不到的东西就是浪费
something you don't use is a waste

29
00:00:59,466 --> 00:01:00,566
可很多时候
But a lot of the time

30
00:01:00,566 --> 00:01:02,666
给用不到的人留一个入口
leaving an entrance for those who can't

31
00:01:02,666 --> 00:01:04,566
反而是更聪明的做法
is actually the smarter move

32
00:01:05,066 --> 00:01:06,866
这条视频不讲冷知识
This video isn't about trivia

33
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:08,266
也不拆机器结构
nor about machine parts

34
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:09,666
只打穿一个判断
It drives home one judgment

35
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:11,366
一个东西值不值得做
whether something is worth doing

36
00:01:11,366 --> 00:01:13,500
不能只看多少人天天用
isn't just about how many use it daily

37
00:01:14,066 --> 00:01:15,733
还要看它不在的时候
It's also about whether without it

38
00:01:15,766 --> 00:01:18,300
有没有人会被悄悄关在门外
someone quietly gets shut out

39
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:20,733
我们先把第一个反差讲透
Let's fully unpack the first contrast

40
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:23,600
为什么明明很多机器上的盲文用得很少
Why do makers add Braille to every machine

41
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,433
厂商还要个个都加上
even where it's rarely used

42
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,266
答案特别朴素
The answer is simple

43
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:30,066
因为给所有机器做同一种键盘
because making one kind of keypad for all machines

44
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:32,000
比分两种键盘便宜
is cheaper than making two

45
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:35,166
你想一下工厂是怎么干活的
Think about how a factory works

46
00:01:35,166 --> 00:01:36,600
一套模具开出来
One mold is made

47
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,966
成千上万块键盘一起压
and thousands of keypads are pressed together

48
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:40,666
库存放一种就行
You only stock one type

49
00:01:40,666 --> 00:01:42,433
工人装的时候不用挑
workers don't have to sort them

50
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:44,200
售后换的时候不用分
repairs don't have to tell them apart

51
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:47,400
这是最省事也最省钱的方式
This is the easiest and cheapest way

52
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:49,366
可如果有人提议
But say someone proposes

53
00:01:49,366 --> 00:01:51,333
反正很多地方盲人用不到
blind folks don't use it in most places

54
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:53,700
我们单独做一批不带盲文的
so let's make a batch without Braille

55
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:56,733
听起来好像省了几个点的成本
It sounds like you'd save a few percent

56
00:01:57,566 --> 00:01:58,433
但实际上呢
but in reality

57
00:01:58,433 --> 00:01:59,966
你得多开一套模具
you'd open another mold

58
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:00,966
多管一条产线
run another line

59
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:02,200
多备一种库存
stock another type

60
00:02:02,866 --> 00:02:05,000
工人装错了还得返工
and redo it when workers mix them up

61
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,433
算到最后
In the end

62
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:08,233
为了去掉那几个凸点
to remove those few dots

63
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:11,500
你花的钱比留着它多得多
you spend far more than keeping them

64
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:13,733
这就是第一个反差
That's the first contrast

65
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:14,866
我们直觉里
Our gut says

66
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:17,400
照顾少数人等于额外掏钱
caring for the few means extra spending

67
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:19,233
可在这件事上
But in this case

68
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:21,966
照顾少数人才是省钱的那条路
caring for the few is the cheaper path

69
00:02:22,266 --> 00:02:23,566
把人关在门外
Shutting people out

70
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:25,800
反而要专门花一笔钱去关
actually costs extra money to do

71
00:02:26,233 --> 00:02:27,833
我知道你可能会说
I know you might say

72
00:02:27,833 --> 00:02:29,566
那这不就是顺手而已吗
isn't this just convenient

73
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:31,300
厂商又不是多有爱心
the makers aren't being kind

74
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,400
没错
Right

75
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:34,866
但这恰恰是我想讲的重点
But that's exactly my point

76
00:02:34,866 --> 00:02:37,266
当留一个入口变成默认动作
When leaving an entrance becomes the default

77
00:02:37,266 --> 00:02:38,900
照顾少数人的成本
the cost of caring for the few

78
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:41,766
其实低到几乎可以忽略
is low enough to be almost nothing

79
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:42,933
真正贵的
What's really expensive

80
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:45,400
是你特意把别人挑出去
is deliberately leaving others out

81
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:47,533
很多公共设计都是这个道理
Many public designs work this way

82
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:49,866
地铁站修一部电梯
A subway station builds an elevator

83
00:02:49,866 --> 00:02:52,933
平时好像只有几个老人和推车的人用
usually only a few elderly and people with carts use it

84
00:02:53,366 --> 00:02:54,166
可你算算
But do the math

85
00:02:54,166 --> 00:02:55,233
如果没有它
without it

86
00:02:55,233 --> 00:02:59,433
每天要多少人停下来抬轮椅抬婴儿车抬行李
how many people stop daily to lift wheelchairs strollers and luggage

87
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:01,766
要多少工作人员跑过来帮忙
how many staff rush over to help

88
00:03:01,766 --> 00:03:03,966
要堵住多少进站的人流
how much foot traffic gets blocked

89
00:03:04,233 --> 00:03:05,800
那笔看不见的麻烦
That invisible hassle

90
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,333
比一部电梯的电费贵多了
costs far more than the elevator's electricity

91
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,800
所以别再用人数多少
So stop using head counts

92
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,933
去判断一个设计该不该存在
to judge whether a design should exist

93
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:13,633
有些入口
Some entrances

94
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:15,700
留着比拆掉更划算
are cheaper to keep than to remove

95
00:03:16,133 --> 00:03:17,166
只是这笔账
It's just that this bill

96
00:03:17,233 --> 00:03:18,733
平时没人替你算
nobody usually adds up for you

97
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,033
现在我们往里再走一层
Now let's go one layer deeper

98
00:03:22,033 --> 00:03:24,266
盲文按键到底解决了什么
What do Braille keys really solve

99
00:03:24,266 --> 00:03:25,166
很多人以为
Many think

100
00:03:25,166 --> 00:03:26,833
它解决的是看不见
they solve not being able to see

101
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:28,900
让盲人能摸出数字
letting blind people feel out numbers

102
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:30,733
这只说对了一半
That's only half right

103
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:31,833
它真正解决的
What they really solve

104
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:33,433
是一个看不见的人
is whether a person who can't see

105
00:03:33,866 --> 00:03:36,366
要不要把自己交到别人手里
has to put themselves in someone else's hands

106
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:38,433
你想象一个画面
Picture this scene

107
00:03:38,966 --> 00:03:39,666
深夜
Late at night

108
00:03:39,666 --> 00:03:42,333
一个视力不好的人站在取款机前
a visually impaired person stands at an ATM

109
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:43,600
旁边没有熟人
no one they know nearby

110
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,400
如果这台机器没有盲文
If the machine has no Braille

111
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:46,533
也没有语音
and no voice guidance

112
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:48,333
他只剩一个选择
they have only one choice

113
00:03:48,333 --> 00:03:49,466
拉住一个陌生人
stop a stranger

114
00:03:49,466 --> 00:03:50,833
把银行卡递过去
hand over the bank card

115
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:52,200
把密码念出来
say the PIN out loud

116
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:54,733
然后赌对方是个好人
and bet the other person is good

117
00:03:55,433 --> 00:03:56,333
你愿意吗
Would you do it

118
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:57,200
你站在那儿
Standing there

119
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,133
把六位密码大声告诉一个不认识的人
saying your six-digit PIN aloud to a stranger

120
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:01,600
光是想想
Just imagining it

121
00:04:02,166 --> 00:04:03,833
你后背都会发凉
sends a chill down your spine

122
00:04:04,566 --> 00:04:06,000
这就是第二个反差
That's the second contrast

123
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,166
我们习惯把帮助当成善良
We tend to see help as kindness

124
00:04:08,166 --> 00:04:09,566
觉得有人愿意搭把手
someone willing to lend a hand

125
00:04:09,566 --> 00:04:10,766
就是温暖的事
feels warm

126
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:13,133
可对被帮的那个人来说
But for the person being helped

127
00:04:13,133 --> 00:04:14,366
每一次求助
every request for help

128
00:04:14,666 --> 00:04:16,100
都是一次暴露
is an exposure

129
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,833
密码暴露
The PIN exposed

130
00:04:17,833 --> 00:04:18,866
余额暴露
the balance exposed

131
00:04:18,866 --> 00:04:20,400
取了多少钱暴露
how much was withdrawn exposed

132
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:23,533
还要欠下一份不知道怎么还的人情
plus a favor you don't know how to repay

133
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,533
钱这件事特别敏感
Money is especially sensitive

134
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:26,666
它连着隐私
It ties to privacy

135
00:04:26,666 --> 00:04:27,700
连着安全
to safety

136
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:30,833
连着一个人能不能自己说了算
to whether a person stays in control of their life

137
00:04:31,566 --> 00:04:33,566
所以盲文按键不是装饰
So Braille keys aren't decoration

138
00:04:33,566 --> 00:04:34,966
它更像一条边界
They're more like a boundary

139
00:04:34,966 --> 00:04:37,133
它让一个人可以少求人一次
They let a person ask for help one less time

140
00:04:37,133 --> 00:04:38,366
少暴露一次
expose themselves one less time

141
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:40,600
少在被帮忙的时候
and when being helped

142
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,600
低一次头
lower their head one less time

143
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:43,166
你可能会说
You might say

144
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:44,866
那靠好心人不行吗
can't we just rely on kind people

145
00:04:44,866 --> 00:04:46,433
世上还是好人多
there are still more good people

146
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,733
好心人当然珍贵
Kind people are precious

147
00:04:49,433 --> 00:04:52,333
但你不能把一个人每个月的取钱
But you can't stake someone's monthly cash withdrawal

148
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:55,533
全押在刚好有好人路过这件事上
on a good person happening to walk by

149
00:04:55,966 --> 00:04:57,200
今天有人帮
Today someone helps

150
00:04:57,266 --> 00:04:58,133
明天呢
what about tomorrow

151
00:04:58,133 --> 00:04:59,133
下雨天呢
what about rainy days

152
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:00,566
半夜呢
what about midnight

153
00:05:01,033 --> 00:05:02,266
一个成熟的系统
A mature system

154
00:05:02,266 --> 00:05:04,400
不是天天指望好心人出现
doesn't count on kind people every day

155
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,800
而是让人本来就不必开口求
it lets people not have to ask in the first place

156
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:09,033
它把那种碰运气的善意
It takes that hit-or-miss goodwill

157
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:12,200
变成了一个稳稳在那儿的入口
and turns it into an entrance that's always there

158
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:13,400
你需要的时候
When you need it

159
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:14,866
它就在
it's there

160
00:05:14,866 --> 00:05:16,266
不需要你证明什么
no need to prove anything

161
00:05:16,266 --> 00:05:17,800
也不需要你欠谁
no need to owe anyone

162
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:20,033
讲到这里
At this point

163
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:21,466
你可能还是觉得
you might still feel

164
00:05:21,466 --> 00:05:23,966
这些设计说到底是给少数人的
these designs are ultimately for the few

165
00:05:23,966 --> 00:05:25,200
跟我关系不大
not much to do with me

166
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:28,500
那我们来看一组你天天在用的东西
Then look at a set of things you use every day

167
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:31,000
商场和地铁里的那种坡道
The ramps in malls and subways

168
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,433
最早是给坐轮椅的人修的
were first built for wheelchair users

169
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:35,166
可你观察一下
But take a look

170
00:05:35,733 --> 00:05:37,500
现在用得最多的是谁
who uses them most now

171
00:05:38,066 --> 00:05:40,200
是拖着拉杆箱赶高铁的你
It's you dragging a suitcase to catch a train

172
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:41,666
是推婴儿车的妈妈
the mom pushing a stroller

173
00:05:41,666 --> 00:05:43,833
是送外卖蹬着小车的小哥
the delivery rider on a little cart

174
00:05:43,833 --> 00:05:45,766
是搬着一箱水的快递员
the courier hauling a case of water

175
00:05:46,233 --> 00:05:48,566
坐轮椅的人反而是少数
Wheelchair users are actually the minority

176
00:05:49,133 --> 00:05:50,266
再看视频字幕
Now look at video subtitles

177
00:05:50,266 --> 00:05:52,633
它一开始是给听障者准备的
They were first made for the hearing impaired

178
00:05:52,966 --> 00:05:53,833
可现在呢
but now

179
00:05:53,833 --> 00:05:55,666
地铁太吵你开字幕
the subway's too loud so you turn them on

180
00:05:55,666 --> 00:05:57,800
深夜怕吵到家人你开字幕
afraid of waking family at night so you turn them on

181
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,300
一边洗碗一边看你开字幕
washing dishes while watching so you turn them on

182
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:01,566
几乎所有人
Almost everyone

183
00:06:01,566 --> 00:06:04,700
都在用一个本来不是为自己设计的东西
uses something that wasn't designed for them

184
00:06:05,333 --> 00:06:07,200
还有手机里的语音播报
And voice navigation on your phone

185
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,233
最早是给看不见的人用的
was first made for those who can't see

186
00:06:09,233 --> 00:06:10,533
可你开车的时候
but when you're driving

187
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,500
不也全靠它在十字路口喊你左转右转吗
don't you rely on it to call out turns at intersections

188
00:06:15,033 --> 00:06:16,633
这就是第三个反差
That's the third contrast

189
00:06:16,633 --> 00:06:19,000
你以为这是一扇为别人开的小门
You think it's a small door opened for others

190
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,333
结果挤进来的
but who crowds through it

191
00:06:20,633 --> 00:06:22,600
全是像你一样的普通人
is ordinary people just like you

192
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:24,933
这个现象有个专门的说法
This phenomenon has a name

193
00:06:24,933 --> 00:06:26,766
叫路缘坡道效应
the curb-cut effect

194
00:06:27,266 --> 00:06:28,133
意思是
It means

195
00:06:28,133 --> 00:06:30,666
当你认真去解决少数人的难处
when you seriously solve the few's difficulties

196
00:06:30,666 --> 00:06:33,933
你常常顺手就把所有人的难处一起解决了
you often solve everyone's difficulties along the way

197
00:06:33,933 --> 00:06:35,333
原因其实不难懂
The reason isn't hard

198
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:37,233
为最困难的那个人设计
designing for the hardest case

199
00:06:37,233 --> 00:06:39,600
会逼着你把东西做得特别清楚
forces you to make things very clear

200
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:40,466
特别简单
very simple

201
00:06:40,466 --> 00:06:42,000
特别不需要解释
needing no explanation

202
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:44,600
而清楚简单不需要解释
And clear simple and self-explanatory

203
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:46,400
是每个人都想要的
is what everyone wants

204
00:06:46,633 --> 00:06:49,033
一个老人也能一眼看懂的提示
A prompt even an elder understands at a glance

205
00:06:49,033 --> 00:06:51,200
赶时间的年轻人扫一眼就走
a rushed young person reads and moves on

206
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:53,233
一个盲人能听明白的语音
A voice a blind person can follow

207
00:06:53,233 --> 00:06:55,633
开车的人也能腾出眼睛看路
lets a driver keep their eyes on the road

208
00:06:55,966 --> 00:06:57,333
所以照顾少数人
So caring for the few

209
00:06:57,333 --> 00:06:59,166
很多时候不是拖慢效率
often isn't slowing things down

210
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:00,400
而是在帮你把事情
it helps you make things

211
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,733
做到连最不方便的人都能完成
work even for the least able

212
00:07:02,733 --> 00:07:03,833
那剩下的人
And everyone else

213
00:07:04,233 --> 00:07:05,433
自然轻松
has it easy

214
00:07:06,233 --> 00:07:09,433
下次你再看到一个好像很多余的设计
Next time you see a design that seems pointless

215
00:07:09,433 --> 00:07:11,000
先别急着说没用
don't rush to call it useless

216
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,033
它很可能
chances are

217
00:07:12,566 --> 00:07:14,166
正悄悄帮着你
it's quietly helping you

218
00:07:15,166 --> 00:07:18,266
最后我想说一个最容易被忽略的反差
Finally the most overlooked contrast

219
00:07:18,266 --> 00:07:20,600
我们都默认自己是正常用户
We all assume we're normal users

220
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:21,233
看得见
we can see

221
00:07:21,233 --> 00:07:21,933
走得快
we walk fast

222
00:07:21,933 --> 00:07:22,733
脑子清楚
we think clearly

223
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:24,333
什么流程一看就会
we get any process at a glance

224
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:25,166
所以总觉得
So we feel

225
00:07:25,166 --> 00:07:27,266
那些为不方便的人留的入口
those entrances left for the less able

226
00:07:27,266 --> 00:07:28,700
跟自己没关系
have nothing to do with us

227
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:31,800
可你今天的顺手
But your ease today

228
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,866
不是因为你天生比别人强
isn't because you're born stronger

229
00:07:33,866 --> 00:07:35,366
只是因为这个系统
it's just that this system

230
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,800
刚好是按你这种人造的
happens to be built for people like you

231
00:07:38,466 --> 00:07:39,566
人生很长
Life is long

232
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:44,300
没有人能一直待在最方便最清醒最强壮的那个位置上
no one stays forever in the most convenient clear and strong spot

233
00:07:44,933 --> 00:07:45,633
你会老
You'll grow old

234
00:07:45,633 --> 00:07:47,333
会有看不清小字的一天
there'll be a day you can't read fine print

235
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:48,200
你会受伤
You'll get hurt

236
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,600
会有拄着拐杖上不了台阶的一天
a day on crutches unable to climb steps

237
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:51,400
你会有一天
There'll be a day

238
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,033
一手抱着哭闹的孩子
holding a crying child in one arm

239
00:07:53,033 --> 00:07:54,066
一手拖着行李
luggage in the other

240
00:07:54,066 --> 00:07:56,033
站在一个完全陌生的车站
standing in a totally unfamiliar station

241
00:07:56,366 --> 00:07:57,600
又困又急
tired and rushed

242
00:07:58,333 --> 00:07:59,166
那一刻
In that moment

243
00:07:59,966 --> 00:08:02,166
你就是那个需要额外入口的人
you're the one who needs the extra entrance

244
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,266
这就是最后一个反差
That's the last contrast

245
00:08:04,266 --> 00:08:06,566
那些你今天觉得多余的设计
The designs you find pointless today

246
00:08:06,566 --> 00:08:09,200
其实是在替将来某个狼狈的你
are really paving the way ahead

247
00:08:09,566 --> 00:08:10,933
提前留好的路
for a future flustered you

248
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:13,866
所以无障碍从来不是慈善
So accessibility was never charity

249
00:08:13,866 --> 00:08:15,233
它是一份保险
it's insurance

250
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:17,566
今天你觉得坡道用不上
Today you think ramps are useless

251
00:08:17,566 --> 00:08:19,400
明天崴了脚就会感谢它
tomorrow you twist an ankle and thank them

252
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:21,400
今天你觉得大字提醒啰嗦
Today you think large-print reminders are wordy

253
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:23,133
明天在陌生车站赶车
tomorrow rushing in a strange station

254
00:08:23,133 --> 00:08:24,866
就靠那个大字救你一把
that big text saves you

255
00:08:24,866 --> 00:08:27,033
今天你觉得盲文离自己很远
Today you think Braille is far from you

256
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:28,800
明天你带着老人去办业务
tomorrow taking an elder to run errands

257
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:32,166
就会庆幸那台机器有清楚的按键和语音
you'll be glad the machine has clear keys and voice

258
00:08:32,533 --> 00:08:34,533
那普通人能从这块盲文里
So what can an ordinary person

259
00:08:34,533 --> 00:08:35,666
带走点什么呢
take from this Braille

260
00:08:35,666 --> 00:08:37,300
我想就三句话
Just three things

261
00:08:37,533 --> 00:08:38,200
第一
First

262
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:40,466
别急着嘲笑一个你用不到的设计
don't rush to mock a design you don't use

263
00:08:40,466 --> 00:08:41,133
你用不到
you not using it

264
00:08:41,133 --> 00:08:42,400
不代表没人需要
doesn't mean no one needs it

265
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:43,366
你嫌它麻烦
what you find a hassle

266
00:08:43,366 --> 00:08:46,133
可能正是别人能自己生活的那一小步
may be someone's small step toward living independently

267
00:08:46,433 --> 00:08:47,066
第二
Second

268
00:08:47,066 --> 00:08:49,466
把尊重从态度变成结构
turn respect from attitude into structure

269
00:08:49,466 --> 00:08:50,966
态度是会变的
Attitude changes

270
00:08:51,466 --> 00:08:52,366
人会累
people get tired

271
00:08:52,366 --> 00:08:53,066
会忙
get busy

272
00:08:53,066 --> 00:08:53,833
会忘
forget

273
00:08:54,533 --> 00:08:57,533
可一个写进按键里写进流程里的入口
But an entrance built into the keys and the process

274
00:08:57,533 --> 00:08:59,133
不需要谁特别开恩
needs no special favor

275
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:00,733
它就稳稳地在那儿
it just stays steadily there

276
00:09:01,133 --> 00:09:01,833
第三
Third

277
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:02,800
做任何事
in anything you do

278
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,000
别只盯着最顺手的那拨人
don't only watch the most capable people

279
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,166
最顺手的人本来就能完成
the capable ones manage anyway

280
00:09:07,433 --> 00:09:09,766
真正检验你有没有想清楚的
What really tests whether you thought it through

281
00:09:09,766 --> 00:09:11,166
是那个慢一点的人
is the slower person

282
00:09:11,166 --> 00:09:12,200
看不清的人
the one who can't see well

283
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:13,333
第一次来的人
the first-timer

284
00:09:13,333 --> 00:09:14,933
不好意思开口的人
the one too shy to ask

285
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,533
如果连他们都能顺利走完
If even they can get through

286
00:09:17,533 --> 00:09:19,433
才说明你真的做对了
then you really did it right

287
00:09:19,866 --> 00:09:21,566
盲文按键最打动我的
What moves me most about Braille keys

288
00:09:21,566 --> 00:09:22,766
不是它多先进
isn't how advanced they are

289
00:09:22,766 --> 00:09:24,000
而是它很安静
it's how quiet they are

290
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:25,166
它不喊口号
They shout no slogans

291
00:09:25,166 --> 00:09:27,166
也不解释自己有多善良
nor explain how kind they are

292
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:28,966
它只是放在那里
They just sit there

293
00:09:28,966 --> 00:09:30,533
等那个需要它的人
waiting for the one who needs them

294
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:32,166
伸手过来
to reach out

295
00:09:32,866 --> 00:09:34,966
一个社会真正体面的样子
What truly makes a society decent

296
00:09:34,966 --> 00:09:36,966
不是每个人看起来都很强
isn't everyone looking strong

297
00:09:36,966 --> 00:09:38,666
而是当有人不方便时
it's that when someone struggles

298
00:09:38,666 --> 00:09:40,333
他不必先证明自己
they don't first have to prove

299
00:09:40,733 --> 00:09:41,966
值得被照顾
they deserve to be cared for

300
00:09:42,566 --> 00:09:44,266
下一期我们聊陶渊明
Next time we'll talk about Tao Yuanming

301
00:09:44,266 --> 00:09:46,300
很多人以为他是在躺平
many think he was just lying flat

302
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,233
但换个角度看
but from another angle

303
00:09:48,233 --> 00:09:50,466
他做的其实是一件很现代的事
he was doing something very modern

304
00:09:50,466 --> 00:09:52,800
主动把自己人生的固定成本
deliberately cutting the fixed costs

305
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:53,633
降下来
of his own life

306
00:09:54,066 --> 00:09:55,233
我们下期见
See you next time
